    Increase the RabbitMQ healthcheck timeout to 60s.
    
    The master images now ship RabbitMQ 4.2.8 on Erlang/OTP 27. The
    container healthcheck (healthcheck_rabbitmq) runs a sequence of
    rabbitmq-diagnostics subcommands, each of which cold-starts its
    own Erlang CLI VM and connects to the broker. On the lighter
    all-in-one CI nodes that serial chain now takes longer than the
    30s default container healthcheck timeout, so the probe is killed
    mid-run ("Health check exceeded timeout (30s)") even though the
    broker itself reports as fully booted with no alarms. The result
    is a container stuck unhealthy (FailingStreak never recovers),
    which trips the post-deploy sanity checks.
    
    Override only the RabbitMQ healthcheck timeout to 60s, leaving the
    global default_container_healthcheck_timeout untouched so no other
    service's probe budget is affected. The same failure reproduces in
    the "no kerbside" all-in-one variant, confirming this is upstream
    slowness rather than anything kerbside introduces.
    
    Prompt: Michael asked, after we diagnosed the 20260616 daily rebase
    CI failure (PR #1329) down to the RabbitMQ container being marked
    unhealthy because its healthcheck exceeded the 30s timeout on the
    all-in-one nodes, for a patch that raises that specific healthcheck
    timeout to 60 seconds. We first ruled out larger CI nodes as a fix
    because the broker was idle (scheduler run queue 1, ~1% memory) and
    the cost is per-invocation Erlang CLI latency, not resource
    starvation.
    
